  "prediction": "**Oldest Cold Case Ever Brought to Court: Man Released After 58 Years**\n\nIn a historic turn of events, Jack McCullough, a 76-year-old man from Washington state, has been released from prison after serving a life sentence for the 1957 murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph. The case, which went unsolved for half a century, was one of the oldest cold cases in the US to be brought to trial.\n\nMaria Ridulph was abducted on December 3, 1957, while playing in the snow with a friend in Sycamore, Illinois. Her body was found five months later, and despite a massive investigation by the FBI, no one was ever charged in connection with her murder.\n\nIn 2012, Jack McCullough was convicted of Maria's murder and sentenced to life in prison. However, in a stunning reversal, DeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack conducted a six-month review of the evidence and concluded that McCullough was wrongly convicted.\n\nSchmack's review found that McCullough's long-held alibi, that he was in Rockford, Illinois, attempting to enlist with the US Air Force at the time of Maria's disappearance, was supported by newly discovered phone records. The records showed that McCullough had made a collect call to his parents at 6:57 p.m. from a phone booth in downtown Rockford, which is 40 miles northwest of where Maria was abducted.\n\nThe judge, William P. Brady, ruled that McCullough would be released from prison, citing the new evidence and the fact that it would have been impossible for McCullough to have been in two places at once at the time of Maria's disappearance.\n\nMaria's family remains convinced that McCullough is guilty and has sought the appointment of a special prosecutor to keep him behind bars. However, the decision to release McCullough has been hailed as a major victory for justice and a testament to the importance of reviewing old cases and re-examining evidence.\n\nMcCullough's release comes after a long and complex investigation that spanned over 50 years. The case has raised questions about the reliability of eyewitness testimony and the importance of physical evidence in securing convictions. It also highlights the need for prosecutors to re-examine old cases and to consider new evidence that may have been overlooked or ignored in the past.\n\nIn a statement, Schmack said that he was confident that McCullough was innocent and that the new evidence proved his",
